## Auto-Bid vs. Manual Bid Strategy

Upwork's auto-bid only fires on jobs open to **worldwide talent**. Any post restricted to **US-only** has to be bid manually. That's actually a feature, not a bug — US-only posts tend to be higher quality, less competitive, and worth a tailored proposal. The strategy splits into two lanes.

### Lane 1: Auto-Bid (Worldwide-Open Posts)

Auto-bid is a volume play. It only works if the trust filter is strict enough that mediocre clients can't slip through. If even one of these criteria fails, the post should not be auto-bid.

**Required client criteria for auto-bid eligibility:**

- **Client location:** US (preferred), UK, CA, AU
- **Budget:** Hourly rate ≥ $90, OR fixed-price ≥ $1,500
- **Required freelancer JSS in the post:** 90%+ (a serious, quality-conscious buyer asks for this)
- **Client rank / risk:** Medium or higher

**"Medium+ client rank" decomposes to:**

- ✅ Payment method verified — required, no exceptions
- ✅ Phone number verified — required for auto-bid
- ✅ At least 3 prior reviews on hires (proves they close, not just post)
- ✅ Average hire amount ≥ $1,000 (real spenders, not test posters)
- ✅ Hire rate ≥ 50% (active hirer, not a tire-kicker — single best signal that the connect won't be wasted)
- ✅ Industry tag: e-commerce, retail, CPG, marketing/advertising, software. Exclude real estate, legal, education-only, government, and local services.

**Additional auto-bid layers worth adding:**

- **Post recency:** Only auto-bid jobs posted within the last 24 hours. Anything older has too much proposal saturation to break through with a templated bid.
- **Connect cost cap:** Auto-bid only on posts costing ≤ 8 connects. Reserve 12–16-connect "premium" posts for manual bids with hand-tailored proposals.
- **Proposal saturation cap:** Skip auto-bidding any job already showing 20+ proposals — the marginal connect doesn't pay back at that depth.
- **Job duration:** Auto-bid on "1–3 months" and "More than 6 months." Skip "Less than 1 month" — those skew Tier C maintenance.
- **Hire history pattern:** Lean in if the client has hired multiple different freelancers in the past 90 days (active program with budget). Deprioritize if the client has hired the same one freelancer 5+ times — they're loyal to that person and a new bidder is filler.
- **Post quality:** Auto-bid only on detailed JDs (≥ 200 words). One-liner posts skew low-quality and tire-kicky.

**Auto-bid kill switches (any one trips → do not auto-bid):**

- Client has any open dispute or a sub-3-star average review
- Post mentions "agency", "white-label", "subcontract", "resell"
- Country is in the bottom-spend tier regardless of other signals
- Post is older than 48 hours
- Job category is outside Marketing / Email / Automation

### Lane 2: Manual Bid (US-Only Posts)

US-only posts are auto-bid's blind spot, but they're often the best opportunities because:

- Less competition — the worldwide pool is excluded
- Clients specifying US usually have higher budgets and more sophistication
- Time-zone overlap is part of what they're paying for, not a bonus
- Many fall straight into Tier A "build & advise" territory

**Treat the US-only feed as the priority queue.** Save it as a dedicated search and review it twice daily (morning and end of day). Hand-write each proposal. Conversion rate should be measurably higher than the auto-bid lane.

**Manual bid prioritization within the US-only queue:**

- **Always priority bid:** Tier A work, Klaviyo migration / audit / strategy, fractional or advisory engagements, $5K+ fixed posts
- **Standard bid:** Tier B builds with clear scope, hourly $90+ with reasonable scope
- **Skip:** Tier C/D, sub-$1.5K fixed, anything below the rubric threshold

### Channel Decision Matrix

| Signal | Auto-Bid | Manual Bid | Skip |
|---|---|---|---|
| Worldwide + meets all auto-bid criteria + ICP fit | ✅ | — | — |
| Worldwide + Tier A work + ICP fit | — | ✅ (hand-tailored) | — |
| US-only + ICP fit | — | ✅ | — |
| Worldwide + fails any auto-bid criterion | — | Only if rubric ≥ 4.0 | — |
| Worldwide + meets criteria but rubric < 3.0 | — | — | ✅ |
| US-only + outside ICP | — | — | ✅ |

### Suggested Auto-Bid Configuration (Starting Point)

- **Daily cap:** 10 bids/day (preserves connects for manual priority bids)
- **Weekly cap:** 40 bids/week
- **Connect spend cap:** Auto-bid consumes no more than 60% of weekly connects. The remaining 40% goes to US-only manual bids where conversion is higher.
- **Pause triggers:** If auto-bid acceptance rate drops below 5% on a rolling 30-day window, pause and re-tighten the filter before resuming.

### Edge Cases Worth Building Filters Around

The standard filter misfires on a handful of recurring scenarios. Watch for these and route them to the right lane manually:

- **"New on Upwork" but verified, deep pockets:** No reviews yet, but payment verified and a serious JD. Auto-bid will skip them. Worth a manual look — these are sometimes legitimate $5M brands posting their first Upwork job.
- **Brand-name clients:** If a recognizable Shopify Plus / DTC brand posts, bypass the rubric and bid manually regardless of score.
- **Re-posts:** Same client posting a similar job 2–3 times suggests they've been burned by previous freelancers. Often a Tier B "build it right this time" — strong opportunity for a confident, opinionated proposal.
- **Invitation-to-interview:** Always priority manual, regardless of channel. The client already filtered for you.
- **Long-running hourly contracts (10+ hires of the same archetype):** Often agencies in disguise. Skip.
- **End-of-quarter post spikes:** Brands have leftover budget in March / June / September / December. If ICP-keyword post volume spikes those months, temporarily widen the auto-bid filter (e.g., drop hire-rate floor from 50% to 40%).
- **Massive single-hire whales ($100K+ on one freelancer):** Won't switch easily. Auto-bid will eat connects with no return. Skip unless the JD explicitly says "looking for someone new."
- **Government / nonprofit / education posts:** Often look ICP-aligned (DTC-flavored language) but pay slowly and procure differently. Skip both lanes.

